          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmology
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#General"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            changes from place to place within our
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            %em beyond
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            (for immediate or not so immediate notions of “beyond”) eventually even the laws of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            might change from place to place, possibly altogether, but at least as concerns the values of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constants"} coupling constants
            or even
            = succeed "-content." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
          %p
            The idea remains hypothetical, naturally, and might ultimately be empty as an idea about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            as opposed to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/philosophy"} philosophy
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmology
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copernican_Revolution"} Copernican revolution
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/galaxies"} galaxies
            etc., to the realization that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            is immense and varied and our physical place and whereabouts inside it just a random result of chance, subject only to the tautological condition that it allows us to be here at all – known as the “anthropic principle” in these discussions.
          %p
            Accordingly, one motivation for the idea of the multiverse is as an explanation for apparent ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/naturalness"}> fine-tuning
            ’ in the “constants of nature”, in particular concerning the presence of complex life. The thought here is that were the constants/laws of nature to be ever so slightly different from what they are, then the universe would not have been to able to evolve to support complex, intelligent life forms (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2017/entries/fine-tuning/"} SEP: Fine-tuning
          %p
            Another motivation of contemplating a multiverse, even in the face of its ontological elusiveness, comes from attempts of formulating fundamental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theories of physics
            such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theories+of+everything"} theories of everything
          %p
            For example, if a fundamental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+everything"} theory of everything
            were to
            = succeed "," do
              %em predict
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generations+of+fundamental+particles"} generations of fundamental particles
            and the values of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yukawa+couplings"} Yukawa couplings
            in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
          %p
            Part of the interest in the idea of the multiverse derives from the feeling that this scenario of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field content
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constants"} coupling constants
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Johannes+Kepler"} Johannes Kepler
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Platonic+solids"} Platonic solids
            (the old
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Music+of+the+Spheres"} Music of the Spheres
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/random+variable"} random
            initial conditions.
          %p
            In this sense, the idea of the multiverse is a form of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empiricism"} empiricism
            and in contrast to
            %span.newWikiWord
              theoretical aestheticism
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/theoretical+aestheticism"} ?
            such as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+exceptionalism"} universal exceptionalism
          %h3#General General
          %p
            On the very largest scales observable in astrophysical
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRW+model"} FRW model
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
            and with primordial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+inflation"} cosmic inflation
            (called the
            = succeed "-CDM" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
            = succeed "\u201D)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} concordance model of cosmology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horizon"} horizon
            in there and everything on smaller scales is the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
          %p
            There has never been a reason to assume that beyond this
            %em cosmic horizon
            visible to us today, the cosmos would not extend further. In fact in the simple
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRW+models"} FRW models
            with positive cosmological constant, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            is not a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed manifold
            and extends indefinitely beyond our observable horizon. This is directly analogous to the ancient mariner who would stand on the deck of his ship and see a few miles of ocean around him. That was the world within his horizon, but there was no telling what lay beyond.
          %p
            Therefore it is important to distinguish the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            from the
            %em universe
            as such. Unfortunately, intellectual laziness tended to ignore this distinction and at some point some people who said “universe” to mean just the stretch of our cosmic horizon felt the need to have a new term for whatever may lie beyond. For better or worse, that new term has become wide-spread these days and is “multiverse”.
          %h3#in_view_of_cosmic_inflation In view of cosmic inflation
          %p
            The main scientific observation that goes with this is the observation that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+inflation"} cosmic inflation
            – for which there is by now excellent and ever increasing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experimental
            evidence that indeed it happened – does, at least by the simple method which is currently used to model it, naturally predict a large ambient space in which local regions perpetually undergo inflationary expansion –
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eternal+inflation"} eternal inflation
          %p
            Hence what is called
            %em multiverse
            these days might more sanely have been called
            %em universe with multiple observable regions
            or the like, but the term has seen an immense increase in popularity and is apparently here to stay.
          %h3#in_view_of_moduli_fields In view of moduli fields
          %p
            Another reason why the old speculation (going back at least to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giordano+Bruno"} Giordano Bruno
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            is but one of many variants in a larger ambient cosmos received more empathetic attention in some corners of fundamental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            at the beginning of the 21st century is that it was argued to lead to a plausible picture of the apparent lack of the fundamental constants of nature (such as notably the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            but also the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yukawa+couplings"} Yukawa couplings
            etc.) to follow any discernible law (see also the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hierarchy+problem"}> hierarchy problem
            ”).
          %p
            At the same time there are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theories
            of fundamental physics where such apparent “constants” of nature are in fact just the values of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+fields"}> moduli fields
            ”, hence of dynamical
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stabilization"}> moduli stabilization
            ”). Generically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} Kaluza-Klein theories
            have this property, and in particular
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            does. This has the remarkable consequence that in such theories the “constants of nature” may dynamically move through their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            – hence through the “landscape” of possible sets of laws of nature – just like any other field evolves dynamically throughout
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory+FAQ"} string theory FAQ
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "string+theory+FAQ#WhatDoesItMeanToSayStringTheoryHasALandscapeOfSolutions"} What does it mean that string theory has a “landscape” of solutions?
          %p
            When the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape of string theory vacua
            was (finally) realized to, apparently, have no reason to be “small”, hence when it was realized that indeed the theory alone did not constrain its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+fields"} moduli fields
            but allowed all kinds of values for them, then it was argued (first in (
            %a{:href => "#Schellekens98"}> Schellekens 98
            )) that combining this apparent embarrassment of a surplus of theoretically admissible “kinds of universes” with the apparent embarrassment of a surplus of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+inflation"} cosmically inflating
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universes
            together yields a plausible picture: each inflating “bubble” in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eternal+inflation"} eternal inflation
            randomly has its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+fields"} moduli fields
            determined by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+fluctuation"} vacuum fluctuation
            that gives rise to the inflationary process, and hence literally the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            of “constants/laws of nature” becomes inhabited by a kind of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+process"} stochastic process
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            or the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yukawa+couplings"} Yukawa couplings
            which we observe would be just as random (or just as “anthropically” constrained) as, say, the number, nature and distance of the planets in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solar+system"} solar system
          %p Whatever the status of this story, it is to a large part responsible for the renewed interest in the idea of the “multiverse”.
          %p
            One must beware here that, while
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+theory"} Kaluza-Klein theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            constitute concrete theoretical frameworks realizing the concept of dynamically evolving “constants of nature”, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape of string theory vacua
            is poorly understood in detail, even by the standards of non-rigorous physics-style arguments; and, worse,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            itself is fully understood only
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbatively
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effect"} non-perturbative completion
            (working title: “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"}> M-theory
            ”, “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"}> F-theory
            ”), despite a large and tight network of hints, remains an open problem, for the time being. But it is exactly
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effects"} non-perturbative effects
            that may be expected to be relevant for true understanding of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape of string theory vacua
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+exceptionalism"} universal exceptionalism
            instead of a multiverse remains a possibility in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
          %h2#critique Critique
          %p
            From (
            %a{:href => "#Ellis11"}> Ellis 11
            ):
          %blockquote
            %p Similar claims [about a multiverse] have been made since antiquity by many cultures. What is new is the assertion that the multiverse is a scientific theory, with all that implies about being mathematically rigorous and experimentally testable. I am skeptical about this claim. I do not believe the existence of those other universes has been proved—or ever could be. Proponents of the multiverse, as well as greatly enlarging our conception of physical reality, are implicitly redefining what is meant by ‘science.’—pg 39
            %p For a cosmologist, the basic problem with all multiverse proposals is the presence of a cosmic visual horizon. The horizon is the limit to how far away we can see, because signals traveling toward us at the speed of light (which is finite) have not had time since the beginning of the universe to reach us from farther out. All the parallel universes lie outside our horizon and remain beyond our capacity to see, now or ever, no matter how technology evolves. In fact, they are too far away to have had any influence on our universe whatsoever. That is why none of the claims made by multiverse enthusiasts can be directly substantiated. —pg 40-41
            %p A remarkable fact about our universe is that physical constants have just the right values needed to allow for complex structures, including living things. Steven Weinberg, Martin Rees, Leonard Susskind and others contend that an exotic multiverse provides a tidy explanation for this apparent coincidence: if all possible values occur in a large enough collection of universes, then viable ones for life will surely be found somewhere. This reasoning has been applied, in particular, to explanation the density of the dark energy that is speeding up the expansion of the universe today. I agree that the multiverse is a possible valid explanation for the value of this density; arguably, it is the only scientifically based option we have right now. But we have no hope of testing it observationally. —pg 42
            %p All in all, the case for the multiverse is inconclusive. The basic reason is the extreme flexibility of the proposal: it is more a concept than well-defined theory. Most proposals involve a patchwork of different ideas rather than a coherent whole. The basic mechanism for eternal inflation does not itself cause physics to be different in each domain in a multiverse; for that, it needs to be coupled to another speculative theory. Although they can be fitted together, there is nothing inevitable about it. … Nothing is wrong with scientifically based philosophical speculation, which is what multiverse proposals are. But we should name it for what it is. —pg 43
            %p As skeptical as I am, I think the contemplation of the multiverse is an excellent opportunity to reflect on the nature of science and on the ultimate nature of existence: why we are here… In looking at this concept, we need an open mind, though not too open. It is a delicate path to tread. Parallel universes may or may not exist; the case is unproved. We are going to have to live with that uncertainty. Nothing is wrong with scientifically based philosophical speculation, which is what multiverse proposals are. But we should name it for what it is.
          %h2#RelatedConcepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Vaguely related is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possible+worlds+semantics"} possible worlds semantics
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
                for the formalization of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metaphysics"} metaphysical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/necessity"} necessity
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possibility"} possibility
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#Kripke80"} Kripke 80
                = succeed "\u201D." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possible+worlds+semantics"} possible worlds
            %li
              %p
                Not really related but arguably somewhat analogous is, in the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set-theoretic+multiverse"} set-theoretic multiverse
                of different
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theories"} set theories
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Topos"} Topos
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
                (see at
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
                for more on this).
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Apparently the first mentioning of the idea that a multiverse due to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eternal+inflation"} eternal inflation
            together with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape of string theory vacua
            provides a plausible story for the nature of “constants of nature” is due to
          %ul
            %li#Schellekens98
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bert+Schellekens"}> Bert Schellekens
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Naar een waardig slot
          %p reproduced as
          %ul
            %li#Schellekens06
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bert+Schellekens"}> Bert Schellekens
              ,
              %em The Landscape “avant la lettre”
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/physics/0604134"}> arXiv:physics/0604134
              )
          %p More recent accounts of how the relevant community pictures the situation:
          %ul
            %li#Bousso11
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Raphael+Bousso"}> Raphael Bousso
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The State of the Multiverse: The String Landscape, the Cosmological Constant, and the Arrow of Time
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ctc.cam.ac.uk/stephen70/talks/swh70_bousso.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brian+Greene"}> Brian Greene
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Is our universe the only universe?
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ted.com/talks/brian_greene_why_is_our_universe_fine_tuned_for_life"} web
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrei+Linde"}> Andrei Linde
                ,
                %em A brief history of the multiverse
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.01203"}> arXiv:1512.01203
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeremy+Butterfield"}> Jeremy Butterfield
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Multiverse: a Philosophical Introduction
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.23639"} arXiv:2505.23639
          %p Further and popular expositions include
          %ul
            %li#Ellis11
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/George+Ellis"}> George Ellis
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Does the multiverse really exist?
              %a{:href => "http://www.scientificamerican.com/article/does-the-multiverse-really-exist/"} web
          %p
            For more references see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eternal+cosmic+inflation"} eternal cosmic inflation
          %p Of course a bunch of links are also here:
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multiverse"} Multiverse
          %p though the discussion there seems to show an undue preference of some points of view.
          %p
            “Possible worlds” in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            are discussed in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
          %ul
            %li#Kripke80
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saul+Kripke"}> Saul Kripke
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Naming+and+Necessity"} Naming and Necessity
              (1980)
        .revisedby
